WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.409 --> 00:00:06.609
I bet most of us can identify this monster.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:08.410
Pretty universal right?

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:14.000
It’s the main attraction in the oldest running
film franchise in the world.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.890
Battling robots and space monsters and King
Kong, it’s our most recognizable Japanese

00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:22.189
kaijū, or “strange beast.”

00:00:22.189 --> 00:00:27.510
With Godzilla’s rise come a bunch more of
these “strange beasts”: Mothra, Biollante,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.500
King Ghidorah, Gigan, and Rodan.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:36.300
And we see these monsters on the big screen
in film after film but these giant creatures’

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.940
stories have a strong literary and social
history too.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.900
So let’s talk about the two who get my vote
for king and queen of the kaijū—Godzilla

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:46.070
and Mothra.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:51.820
Why did these two emerge in Japan when they
did and what was their global attraction?

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:01.980
Movies made kaijū world famous.

00:01:01.989 --> 00:01:07.009
We can’t even begin to talk about them without
looking at the film that started the ‘daikaiju

00:01:07.009 --> 00:01:13.350
eiga’ or ‘giant monster film’ genre—the
1954 black and white classic Gojira.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:19.240
The movie directed by Ishirō Honda shows
the modern discovery of a 50-meters-tall dinosaur

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.220
with atomic breath just living its best life
in the depths of the ocean.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.140
At least until an atomic bomb test destroys
its natural habitat —and the monster rises

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.430
to terrorize Japan.

00:01:30.430 --> 00:01:34.569
Many people took Godzilla as an obvious metaphor
for the continuing horrors of the nuclear

00:01:34.569 --> 00:01:39.180
attacks used against Japan by the U.S. during
World War II.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.460
Honda saw first-hand the destruction of war
serving as a soldier in China.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:49.210
The 1945 atomic bombings of Hiroshima and
Nagasaki loomed in the minds of the Japanese

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.450
people.

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.540
The film itself is a social commentary.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.200
So much so that a main character of the film
asks out right “Isn’t Godzilla a product

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.640
of the atomic bomb that still haunts many
of us Japanese?".

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.500
I mean is it even a metaphor at this point?

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.110
Godzilla is radioactive from the testing and
during its rampage thousands of people die

00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:12.610
on the street, hit with Godzilla’s radioactive
breath.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:16.130
Entire neighborhoods are set aflame with a
bright flash.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.380
Given the United State’s role in World War
II it’s not surprising to me that when the

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:26.110
film was introduced in North America in 1956
as Godzilla, King of the Monsters!

00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:29.010
—it had been heavily edited.

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:34.570
Thirty minutes of explicit references to the
atomic bombings and nuclear testing were removed

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:39.210
from the movie and replaced with two pretty
terrible additions— the actor Raymond Burr,

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:52.060
who was very awkwardly spliced into the American
version, and famously bad English dubbing.

00:02:52.060 --> 00:03:00.120
However, key scenes were kept that show the
Japanese as both heroic individuals and sympathetic

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.600
victims.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.690
Culturally, the best thing to come out of
this Westernized version of Gojira was the

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:09.910
introduction of Godzilla and the Japanese
kaijū to a global audience.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:15.180
The film’s international success launched
dozens of movie sequels and we start to see

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.220
the monsters appearing in manga, a distinct
comic style unique to Japan.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:21.870
.
A manga comic featuring Godzilla was released

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.020
the same year as the original film.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:28.760
Reprinted and translated from the original
1985’s The Return of Godzilla, the first

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:33.420
English-translated Godzilla manga wasn’t
released until 1988.

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.550
In this comic Godzilla is woken from slumber
on the ocean floor by a massive volcanic eruption,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.290
not as a result of nuclear testing.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.340
Still it must feed on radioactive substances,
making submarines and nuclear power plants

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:49.870
prime targets for its destruction.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.440
The negative portrayal of nuclear weapons
remains.The series ends with an emotional

00:03:54.440 --> 00:03:59.060
message about humanity’s willingness to
destroy the natural balance of the world and

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:04.240
reminds the reader that some natural force
may rise up to challenge the arrogance of

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:05.240
mankind.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.640
Other comic versions of Godzilla aren’t
so serious, and some are even downright ridiculous.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:14.610
Godzilla: Rage Across Time shows Godzilla
killing the majority of the Greek gods until

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.079
Zeus defeats him.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.790
One of the most recent literary versions of
Godzilla was published as a prequel to the

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:26.030
2014 American film rebooting the franchise,
hitting shelves less than two weeks before

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.100
the movie’s premiere.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:32.320
Godzilla: Awakening explains that Godzilla
is an ancient apex predator lured to the surface

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:34.580
by the radiation left after Hiroshima.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:38.590
This is only implied in the film itself.

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.630
The 2014 Godzilla film marked the beginning
of Legendary’s MonsterVerse.

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:45.650
Which obviously, means more Godzilla.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:50.570
But we know from the new trailers that more
kaijū will return to the big screen, most

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:58.620
importantly—Mothra!

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.960
I love Mothra.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.490
Yes, really, I love Mothra.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:07.560
I would pick her over Godzilla, Mechagodzilla,
even Space Godzilla (although I do love those

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.440
crystal shoulder pads)
Mothra is beautiful!

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:16.970
She’s a brightly colored winged protectress
who, understandably, is worshipped as a goddess.

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:18.880
She shoots a rainbow laser out of her head.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:19.930
She’s awesome.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:25.220
Unlike Godzilla, Mothra first appeared in
literature, in the 1961 serialized novel The

00:05:25.220 --> 00:05:27.280
Luminous Fairies and Mothra.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.000
The story follows a group of humans who discover
Mothra on a South Pacific island where she

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.570
is worshipped by fairies.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:37.650
Two of the female fairies are stolen, and
Mothra goes to Japan to save them.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.720
Which she obviously does.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.699
Because again, Mothra is just that kind of
amazing lady.

00:05:42.699 --> 00:05:46.230
Mothra’s first standalone film hit theaters
the same year.

00:05:46.230 --> 00:05:50.639
In the movie, Mothra hatches from an egg in
the South Pacific and as a caterpillar swims

00:05:50.639 --> 00:05:52.330
to the Japanese shore.

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:57.860
This journey from her island to Japan parallels
that of the real-world influx of goods produced

00:05:57.860 --> 00:05:59.650
in and inspired by the South Pacific.

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:04.840
Bananas, mangos, and Hawaiian print fabrics
made popular by Elvis in Blue Hawaii were

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:09.010
all popular commodities in Japan in the 1960s.

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:14.490
The fact that both the flying kaijū and her
tiny priestesses are female does also suggest

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:18.310
some complicated representations of commodifying
women.

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:22.229
Nevertheless, I appreciate Mothra because
she is one of the few female monsters who

00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:27.590
is not hideous, not typically sexualized,
and has powers and strength to rival any of

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:29.160
her male counterparts.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.160
So yeah, Mothra is queen.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:33.270
And I am not the only one who feels this way.

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:39.630
A 2017 poll of 10,000 Godzilla franchise fans
by Japan’s Asahi television network named

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.440
Mothra their number one favorite Godzilla
monster.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:46.570
She really is “The Queen of Monsters.”

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:51.310
Even when the kaijū are adopted and adapted
by non-Japanese cultures, there is still no

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:56.570
denying that the monsters are influenced by
their predecessors in Japanese cinema and

00:06:56.570 --> 00:06:57.570
literature.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:07:01.100
All kaijū demonstrate the tension between
technology and nature.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:06.479
They suggest that although humanity seeks
progress, innovation and science, there are

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:09.420
serious, potentially cataclysmic side effects.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:15.180
So I’ll end with the words of Dr. Serizawa
from the 2014 Godzilla movie, quote “The

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:20.180
arrogance of man is thinking that nature is
in their control, and not the other way around.”

00:07:52.669 --> 00:07:54.040
Let me know in the comments!

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.139
And don’t forget to subscribe!

